<section id="problem" class="border-t border-line py-20 sm:py-28" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker mb-5"><span class="num">01</span> The problem</p>
    <h2 id="problem-heading" class="font-serif italic font-semibold text-section leading-[1.1] max-w-3xl">
      If the tests that judge an agent are wrong, the leaderboard is too.
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] text-paper/90">
      <p>
        Reinforcement-learning environments and coding benchmarks score a policy by running a
        verifier: a test suite, a grader, a reward function. When those tests accept a wrong
        solution, reject a correct one, or change their verdict from run to run, training and
        evaluation are measuring the wrong thing.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is already documented in public sources. The citations below are from those sources,
        not from an Aethens audit of the same benchmarks — Aethens has not published findings on
        them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <li class="border border-line bg-raised/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-[0.68rem] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-gold">OpenAI, Feb 2026</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-paper/95 leading-relaxed">
          OpenAI explained why it no longer evaluates on SWE-bench Verified. It had audited 27.6% of
          the Verified set and found that at least 59.4% of the audited problems had tests that
          reject functionally correct submissions.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            class="prose-link font-mono text-[0.75rem]"
            href="https://openai.com/index/why-we-no-longer-evaluate-swe-bench-verified/"
          >
            openai.com — retirement post
          </a>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border border-line bg-raised/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-[0.68rem] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-gold">SWE-Bench+, 2024</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-paper/95 leading-relaxed">
          The SWE-Bench+ paper reported that 31.08% of “resolved” patches passed through weak tests,
          and that 32.67% copied solutions leaked in issue text.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a class="prose-link font-mono text-[0.75rem]" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06992">
            arXiv:2410.06992
          </a>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="border border-line bg-raised/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-[0.68rem] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-gold">EvalPlus / HumanEval+</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-paper/95 leading-relaxed">
          EvalPlus’s HumanEval+ adds roughly 80× more tests than the original HumanEval suite —
          public evidence that widely used coding tests can be incomplete. Aethens uses HumanEval+
          only as an outside oracle for its own mutation check.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a class="prose-link font-mono text-[0.75rem]" href="https://github.com/evalplus/evalplus">
            github.com/evalplus/evalplus
          </a>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
